ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Long-horizon agents with external memory/RAG that exhibit moderate-to-high topic reuse (p_repeat >=0.4) and maintain a bounded live context window (W >=10 spans) where recently retrieved content remains resident. Examples: multi-turn conversational assistants, coding agents iterating on the same files, or research agents revisiting prior findings within a session. The gate applies when retrieval latency or injected-token cost is a bottleneck and the agent would otherwise re-fetch content already visible in its window.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only. The 39% skip-at-zero-loss result (C_pareto.csv tau=0.3: savings=0.393, acc_check=1.000) and +9 to +19 pt delta vs random-skip (C_pareto.csv delta CI [0.178,0.190] at tau=0.3) are derived from a constructed trace generator, not real agent/RAG logs. The study explicitly states "THIS IS NOT A REAL-DATA RESULT — it is a sensitivity/characterization study whose contribution is the *framework + the conditional map*, not a universal number" (RESULTS.md). L1 requirements are explicitly listed as: real traces with in-context-presence labels, a real sentence embedding (vs stdlib hashed surrogate), and real end-to-end task accuracy with an LLM in the loop (RESULTS.md Section 6). Without those, the win is conditional on synthetic workload knobs (p_repeat, W, paraphrase_rate) and has not been shown to survive production factors such as paraphrased redundancy, model recovery from wrong skips, or existing caching/dedup layers.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Real-trace validation required—redundancy fraction is workload-dependent (0.235 at p_repeat=0.2/W=5 vs 0.816 at p_repeat=0.8/W=20, A_redundancy_vs_workload.csv) and may be lower in production; Integration complexity—must maintain live window spans and compute overlap per retrieval; Threshold tuning risk—aggressive tau (<=0.1) drops accuracy to 0.741 at 78% skip (C_pareto.csv), so safe operating point requires careful calibration; Lexical check degrades with paraphrase—AUC falls to 0.835 at paraphrase=0.9 (B_check_quality.csv), and the stdlib hashed surrogate is weaker (AUC 0.686); a real embedding would add compute cost, eroding the "cheap" claim; Existing adaptive-retrieval systems (Self-RAG/FLARE/SKR/Adaptive-RAG) already gate on "need external knowledge," and production RAG stacks often have caching/dedup, reducing marginal benefit; Operational risk—wrong skip is modeled as hard task failure; real LLMs might re-ask, but that adds latency, negating savings; No measured end-to-end task accuracy with a real model]
RATIONALE: The packet's L0 verdict is "HELD, conditional" precisely because it is a model-based characterization, not a real-data result (RESULTS.md: "THIS IS NOT A REAL-DATA RESULT... The contribution is the anti-circular framework + the conditional map, not a universal constant"). Redundancy >20% holds only across the "realistic workload band" defined by the generator; at low reuse (p_repeat=0.2, W=5) the fraction is 0.235, approaching the 20% floor (A_redundancy_vs_workload.csv). The cheap check's advantage over random-skip (delta +0.184 CI [0.178,0.190] at tau=0.3, C_pareto.csv) uses a weak baseline and a synthetic task-success model (success iff needed item available). The authors explicitly note that "a real LLM may partially recover from a wrong skip (re-ask) or fail in subtler ways" and that "real injected-token and retrieval-latency costs vary by system" (RESULTS.md Limitations). Prior adaptive-retrieval work already addresses "when to retrieve," and the narrow novelty here (in-context-presence gating) has not been shown to deliver operational savings in a production RAG stack where context windows, caching, or a paged kernel may already make re-fetching cheap. The negative result is actionable: it provides a workload-conditional framework and a matched-random control that future work must beat on real traces.
